[gilbert@BINAH.CC.BRANDEIS.EDU writes:]
[] Suraj Iyer says that both spellings of Grey/Gray are correct,
[] an hypothesises that perhaps the program just doesn't recognise it. . .
[]
[]  Ah, but sir, Grey is incorrect for dragons and Gray is incorrect for
[] elves - you see these are _names_ and while John may be a "correct"
[] spelling, if you wish for one, you will not get the person you wished
[] for if he spells his name "Jon".  :-) :-) :-)

technically correct, morally wrong! as i pointed out before, the game
admits confusion concerning some spellings and in this case introduced
the confusion concerning gray/grey. while it may be difficult to program
a parser to accept both gray/grey such a beast is clearly called for
since the play of the game is the most important thing.

[] As a graphic example (and much to my chagrin)
[] I had a few times wished for a blessed +3 war hammer named Mjolnir.  And I 
[] got exactly that.  I wondered why everyone thought it was so cool until I
[] wondered into the sources and found that _they_ were killing everything in 
[] sight with a blessed +3 war hammer named _Mjollnir_ !!!! :-) :-) :-) 

there are two problems here:
1) not everyone will have the sources to correct their mistakes. nor
should they need sources.
2) it seems like an inadvertant cruel joke that you could get a war
hammer named Mjolnir which is simply a war hammer altho you might
believe it is better because of how you obtained it.

if i could get my wishes i'd have the wish parser start very simple:
"you may wish for an item!"
"what type of item would you like?" [[!?"=*%$`()0] <type . for help>
{the player selects an item type, let's say ")".}
"what type of weapon would you like?"
{a tabulated list of real weapons (i.e. broadsword, katana) is displayed}
{the player selects the index corresponding to one of these}
{let's say he chooses: u - a long sword}
{since there are special long swords another message}
"what type of long sword would you like?"
a - long sword
b - long sword named Demonbane
c - long sword named Excalibur
d - long sword named Fire Brand
e - long sword named Frost Brand
f - long sword named Sunsword
g - long sword named Werebane
{let's say he chooses: f - a long sword named Sunsword}
"what is the karma of your long sword named Sunsword?"
a - blessed
b - uncursed
c - cursed
{choose a - blessed}
"how many enchantments would you like?" [-5...+5] +5
{you get}
"the gods have decided to grant you:"
m - a blessed +0 long sword named Sunsword

this form of wishing doesn't require experience and has certain
other advantages (i.e. a simple logic tree) and i don't think it
gives away too much and more importantly, i believe, it plays
right.

